1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,500
It's not great.

2
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:02,140
All right.

3
00:00:02,540 --> 00:00:04,560
So, questions about coordination?

4
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:05,780
All right.

5
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:08,620
So, a useful game for thinking about the real world,

6
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:11,780
even if obviously those exact payoffs are just invented.

7
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:12,960
Yes?

8
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:16,880
It seems like Menger's theory of money is similar.

9
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:17,500
Yeah, of course.

10
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,160
Yeah, so that's actually one, you know, that's not just that,

11
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:23,980
but it's very much like the why mixed strategies aren't stable.

12
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:25,880
So, you know, Karl Menger has this theory

13
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:26,900
about the emergence of money.

14
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:29,920
And the idea is that even if initially you have a bunch

15
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,140
of different commodities that are being used as money,

16
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:34,900
still people gravitate towards the one that is most used.

17
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:37,500
And once people start doing that, then it's even better

18
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:38,820
to be the most used, and even better,

19
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:40,620
and the other ones gradually disappear.

20
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:42,960
In a way, what's striking is

21
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:45,300
that there's not yet a world currency by this process.

22
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:46,660
Well, there was gold.

23
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:46,800
Yes.

24
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,820
And we're going that way.

25
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,780
But even with gold, people still generally transacted

26
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:51,960
in national denominations.

27
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,340
And it's been noted by macroeconomists

28
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:59,340
who study hyperinflation that normally all you need

29
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:01,560
to revive a crummy domestic currency is just

30
00:01:01,560 --> 00:01:02,500
to stop hyperinflation.

31
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,960
You might think that after you have a big hyperinflation

32
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:09,560
and the economy dollarizes, it would stay dollarized.

33
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:11,280
But normally it doesn't work that way.

34
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:12,880
Normally, if you just get inflation back

35
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:15,340
down to 20% per year, people switch back

36
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:17,760
to the crummy currency and away from the dollar again.

37
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:20,120
Again, probably part of that is just the government wants its

38
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:22,080
taxes in local currency and things like that.

39
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,020
But there's also something very focal in the modern world

40
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:25,900
about your nation.

41
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:28,740
And people go, what nation am I on?

42
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:29,700
What nation am I in again?

43
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:30,460
I'm in Zimbabwe.

44
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:30,720
All right.

45
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:34,640
Well, is the Zimbabwean dollar the worst thing in the world?

46
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:35,940
No? All right.

47
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:37,060
I guess I'll use it then.

48
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,840
Whereas if it is the worst thing in the world,

49
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:39,840
then different story.

50
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:41,200
All right.

51
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:43,640
So, all right.

52
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:45,620
Let's move on to bargaining.

53
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:46,680
All right.

54
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:49,660
So let's think about a simple model of bargaining.

55
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:51,280
We've already talked about this a little bit,

56
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:53,080
but worth pursuing it in some more depth.

57
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:54,740
All right.

58
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:58,060
So. All right.

59
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:00,700
So, figure you've got two bargaining strategies.

60
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:02,640
You can be a hard bargainer or a soft bargainer.

61
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:10,680
All right.

62
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:12,440
And you probably know which one you are.

63
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,640
Right? And if you end up with kids,

64
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:15,940
you can also generally see,

65
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:17,940
kids generally gravitate towards hard bargaining.

66
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,820
Almost as if it's a dominant strategy,

67
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:21,820
which as we'll see it's not.

68
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,860
All right.

69
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:26,700
Or it also is if there's not a repeated game, which it is.

70
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,100
So, possibly children aren't that strategic.

71
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:30,900
But in any case.

72
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:31,540
All right.

73
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:33,540
So, suppose we have a set up like this

74
00:02:33,540 --> 00:02:36,180
where if both people bargain hard, then the payoffs are zero

75
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:38,640
because you can't reach a deal if both parties bargain hard.

76
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:42,780
So, both players are aggressive bargainers who ask for a lot.

77
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:45,620
If both sides ask for more than half a surplus,

78
00:02:46,120 --> 00:02:48,860
then there will be no deal because you cannot give

79
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,820
everybody more than 100% of the surplus.

80
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,160
Some surplus cannot exceed 100.

81
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:55,800
And then suppose that if one party bargains hard

82
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:57,460
and the other one bargains soft,

83
00:02:57,800 --> 00:02:59,740
then the hard bargainer gets most of the surplus

84
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,060
but the soft bargainer gets something.

85
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:05,300
And same thing if player one is the soft bargainer,

86
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:06,780
then player one gets the bad payoff

87
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:08,360
and player two gets the good one.

88
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:09,660
Although still more than nothing.

89
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:12,840
Then finally, suppose the best one

90
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:14,380
of all is both players bargain soft.

91
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:17,660
And so, you read the introduction to Freakonomics.

92
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,300
At least it sounds like a great partnership

93
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:21,300
between Dubner and Levitt.

94
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,260
They said that, well, when we started,

95
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:26,020
we started discussing how to divide the royalties,

96
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:28,340
each of us said 60%.

97
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:31,000
And then finally we realized each one was offering the other

98
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:31,860
with 60%.

99
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:34,100
Oh, it's so stupid.

100
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:34,380
All right.

101
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:35,940
And they made millions.

102
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:36,700
All right.

103
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,060
Probably tens of millions actually.

104
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:40,060
All right.

105
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,520
So, very good.

106
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,160
So, that's a case where they're both soft bargainers,

107
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,040
each offering the other one more than half.

108
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:48,380
And that way the deal definitely happens.

109
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:49,700
All right.

110
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:50,360
All right.

111
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:52,480
Now, if you take a look at this in terms of pure strategies,

112
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,540
we got two pure strategies here.

113
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:56,220
Right. So, you get a kiss here.

114
00:03:56,220 --> 00:03:58,040
If you're at hard, then obviously someone wants

115
00:03:58,040 --> 00:03:59,140
to stop being so hard.

116
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:02,040
And here, both people have delayed both players

117
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:02,860
from bargaining soft.

118
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:05,560
There's incentive for each player's incentive to go

119
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:07,840
and say, hey, well, if you're going to be so soft, then yeah,

120
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:08,540
give me more stuff.

121
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:08,960
I want more.

122
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:11,500
All right.

123
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:13,480
But there's also a mixed strategy

124
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:14,900
in Nash equilibrium here.

125
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:17,920
And if you solve it out, you'll see it's.5.5.

126
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:21,760
And several interesting things about it.

127
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:24,220
First one is that this one is stable.

128
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:27,360
This is not like that coordination game one

129
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,500
where some of the 51% of people are playing Hebrew.

130
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:30,640
You want to play Hebrew too.

131
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:32,220
Actually, this one is the opposite.

132
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:34,560
This is one where 51% of people are playing hard.

133
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:35,740
You want to play soft.

134
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:40,080
Why? Because once the percentage playing hard gets

135
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:42,680
over 50%, then you're actually having,

136
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:45,020
you have too high a probability of that bad outcome.

137
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:47,400
And then being the person

138
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:50,820
that bargains soft actually raises your payoff in a world

139
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:51,780
where people are bargaining hard.

140
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:54,620
And again, just imagine that everyone is bargaining hard.

141
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:56,280
What do you, you want to be the one person

142
00:04:56,280 --> 00:04:57,120
in the world who does what?

143
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:00,060
Bargain soft.

144
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:01,660
Everyone else is being a jerk.

145
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:04,000
Everyone else is saying, I want 60%.

146
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:04,900
No, I want 60%.

147
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:06,440
I will let us settle this at 60%.

148
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:09,660
And you be the one person who says 40% is fine by me.

149
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:11,440
And then you make a deal with everybody.

150
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:13,580
And then everyone is your customer.

151
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:16,320
And then you make way more than everybody else makes.

152
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,060
That's a more extreme case.

153
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,960
But actually, these numbers you are like, you know,

154
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:23,080
like you actually, at least you get a deal rather than no deals,

155
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:24,640
which is what all the hard bargainers are getting.

156
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:27,340
And of course, if you were in 100% soft bargaining,

157
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:29,120
then you want to be the one person who's aggressive.

158
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:32,760
And so there's a natural tendency to, for the,

159
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:33,540
for this mixed strategy,

160
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:35,420
natural equilibrium to actually stay around.

161
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:37,580
Right? So it is actually stable.

162
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:40,880
We could do, we could work for the math, but we're not going to.

163
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:43,460
Again, the intuition is all that's really important here.

164
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:46,180
It's realizing that if you were at either extreme,

165
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:47,920
here you would not stay at the extremes.

166
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:51,780
Instead, you would naturally gravitate towards this mixed

167
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:52,680
strategy and natural equilibrium.

168
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:54,020
All right.

169
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:55,920
And again, the intuition is an equilibrium.

170
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:57,540
Both strategies are equally good.

171
00:05:57,980 --> 00:05:59,460
Landsberg has this line saying,

172
00:05:59,460 --> 00:06:01,540
don't mistake a hard bargainer for a good bargainer.

173
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:02,960
Let's see.

174
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:05,300
I think that the whole idea of Trump's art

175
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:07,360
of the deal is a hard bargainer, a good bargainer.

176
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:09,340
A hard bargainer is a good bargainer and vice versa.

177
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:10,480
But that's just wrong.

178
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:14,720
Right? And of course, he himself obviously does not actually

179
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:15,400
really do that.

180
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:19,220
Right? If you've ever had any transaction

181
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,280
with Trump Enterprises, he doesn't just go

182
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,940
and demand $1,000 for every hotel room.

183
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:25,320
Instead, realizes that if he tries bargaining

184
00:06:25,320 --> 00:06:26,740
that hard, he'll just go someplace else.

185
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:29,600
Okay? All right.

186
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:31,060
Now, the outcome that you get

187
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:32,440
with the mixed strategy and natural equilibrium,

188
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:35,520
it is not going to be first best.

189
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:38,600
So if people are playing.5,.5,

190
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:40,840
then 25% of the time you're there.

191
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:42,260
25% of the time you're there.

192
00:06:42,260 --> 00:06:43,560
25% of the time you're there.

193
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:45,420
And 25% of the time you're there.

194
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:49,920
Right? Because you are basically flipping two coins each 50-50.

195
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:53,300
So that's like two heads, two tails, head, tail, tail, head.

196
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:59,260
Okay? Now, if you make this bad outcome worse and worse,

197
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:02,300
then you do make it less and less likely people will play it.

198
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:05,840
So if instead of zero, this were like negative a million

199
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:10,200
for each person, then that outcome does become very rare.

200
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:13,860
Although still, there is a very small risk

201
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:14,900
of a really bad thing happening.

202
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:17,100
Right? And if you put in the numbers,

203
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:18,380
you can see how bad it gets.

204
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:21,900
Now, of course, in this model, you always want people

205
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:23,020
to think you're going to play hard.

206
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:26,040
You always want people to think that you're going to play hard.

207
00:07:26,660 --> 00:07:29,680
But here's the key thing.

208
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:31,900
If everybody wants you to think something about them,

209
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:35,880
then the fact that they are sending out signs

210
00:07:35,880 --> 00:07:38,500
that they're this kind of a person is not very convincing.

211
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:40,420
Just like in a job interview.

212
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:42,980
Everybody in a job interview wants to act

213
00:07:42,980 --> 00:07:44,560
like they're what kind of a worker?

214
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:46,300
A hard worker.

215
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:49,000
So if everyone wants to act like a hard worker,

216
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:52,100
then what actual payoff do you get

217
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:53,460
from acting like a hard worker?

218
00:07:54,340 --> 00:07:56,240
Not much because everybody else is doing it.

219
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:57,760
It isn't very convincing.

220
00:07:57,760 --> 00:07:59,660
Of course, if you were actually going to the interview

221
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:01,920
and to say I'm super lazy, right,

222
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:03,020
then you get treated differently.

223
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:05,420
But that would very rarely happen, as you know.

224
00:08:05,980 --> 00:08:06,180
All right.

225
00:08:06,180 --> 00:08:08,500
So if everyone wants to be perceived as a hard bargainer,

226
00:08:08,740 --> 00:08:10,200
it's very hard to convince anyone

227
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:11,600
that you're actually a hard bargainer.

228
00:08:12,380 --> 00:08:18,780
And as a result, the actual payoff of, you know,

229
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:21,680
the actual reason to be perceived this way is not even

230
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,780
that great because there's no really credible way

231
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:24,460
of convincing people.

232
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:25,560
It's like, well, that's what they all say.

233
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:28,120
So, you know, why do you always tell me that you're great?

234
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:29,220
Something like that.

235
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:35,080
Okay. Now, in this bargaining model, it has a very simple

236
00:08:35,080 --> 00:08:38,460
and yet kind of dissatisfying theory

237
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:40,740
about why people don't reach agreements when it's

238
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:41,900
in their mutual interests.

239
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:44,720
And the answer is bad luck.

240
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:48,440
Bad luck. So if there's two people

241
00:08:48,440 --> 00:08:50,560
and there's a mutually beneficial trade between them

242
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:51,720
and they fail to reach a deal,

243
00:08:52,220 --> 00:08:53,860
the mixed strategy explanation is, well,

244
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:54,900
looks like they got two tails.

245
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,020
And they both play the mixed strategies came

246
00:08:58,020 --> 00:09:00,420
out the bad way, and so there's a bad outcome,

247
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:01,520
and that's the story.

248
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:02,900
All right.

249
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:05,680
So it's kind of dissatisfying, but it makes sense.

250
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:06,520
All right.

251
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:09,020
So if there's randomization in the strategy

252
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:11,400
that people are playing, then when are you going

253
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:16,620
to get both people going and taking the actions that lead

254
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:18,100
to a bad result for both of them?

255
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:20,360
Well, you just have to go and multiply the probabilities

256
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:21,360
of those two strategies together,

257
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:23,200
and that gives you the bad outcome.

258
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:24,540
All right.

259
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,580
So in other words, then, when you see two people

260
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:28,260
who are failing to actually reach a mutually

261
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:30,320
beneficial agreement, it's generally tempting

262
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:31,180
to say they're just stupid.

263
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:34,260
And I will say, yeah, I'm very tempted to say this a lot

264
00:09:34,260 --> 00:09:36,640
of times, like the way my parents still haven't figured

265
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:38,440
out a way to calmly resolve any dispute.

266
